		{
			"identifier": "MaryOPipe",
			"uid": 106908,
			"tags": [],
			"exportToToc": false,
			"allowOutOfBounds": true,
			"doc": "One half of a WARP TUBE — the green pipe you press into and come out of somewhere else.  Two halves that share a `link` are ONE tube. That is the whole pairing, and it is explicit: nothing about where a pipe sits, what it is called, or what order it was drawn in decides who its partner is. A link with only one half is REFUSED at load, naming the pipe and the partner it is missing — which is exactly what the old `warp_pipe_<link>_<up|down>` naming convention could not do, because a typo simply produced a green box that had quietly stopped being a pipe.  `link` — the pair id. Any word you like; both halves must spell it the same. 1-1 uses `descent` (surface down into the vault) and `ascent` (vault back up to the surface).  `mouth` — which way this half's OPEN FACE points:    Up    the lip is on TOP. You stand on it and press DOWN to enter; you arrive standing on it.   Down  the lip is UNDERNEATH — a pipe hanging from a ceiling. You stand under it and press UP to enter; you arrive falling out of it.  A pipe answers UP or DOWN and never a generic Interact, so the mouth is what decides the press. It also decides which end wears the lip art, and the lip is mirrored for a Down mouth so a hanging pipe reads as hanging.  `role` — which end of the trip this is. Each link needs exactly one of each:    Entrance  the mouth you press into.   Exit      the mouth you come out of.  A tube is directed, unlike a portal pair: 1-1's two vault halves are identical boxes hanging in the same ceiling, and `role` is the only thing that says which one you may press UP into. Author a two-way route as two links, the way 1-1 does.  Drag the box to the pipe's real size — the collision, the mouth you touch and the lip/shaft art are all derived from it, so a pipe moved in the editor takes its warp with it.",
